From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bluefin Server maintainers <noreply@projectbluefin.io>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] linux: drop firmware-bound Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and GPU drivers;
 zstd vmlinuz

Bluefin Server ships no /usr/lib/firmware. Wi-Fi (cfg80211, mac80211 and
every WLAN driver), Bluetooth, and the amdgpu, radeon, nouveau and xe GPU
drivers need firmware for all or nearly all of the hardware they drive,
so turn them off. On 26.10.1215 they were ~24 MB of modules in /usr plus
cfg80211, mac80211 and the Bluetooth core built into vmlinux. Consoles
keep simpledrm, i915, virtio-gpu, hyperv_drm, vmwgfx and vboxvideo.

FSDK's `module`/`enable` lines put every option they set in
expected-configs, and linux.bst fails the build on any that olddefconfig
leaves unset. The block therefore records which expected options are set
before it runs, applies the `remove`s, runs olddefconfig, and takes out
of expected-configs exactly the options that went from set to unset (the
build log lists them). Everything else is still checked. Besides the
drivers and their sub-options this drops CEC_CORE/MEDIA_CEC_RC: CEC_CORE
has no prompt and was only selected through CEC_NOTIFIER by amdgpu's
display code.

Switch the vmlinuz compression from gzip to zstd (KERNEL_ZSTD; linux.bst
already build-depends on zstd): ~3 MB smaller in every UKI.

Appended after 0007, whose last three lines are the hunk's context.

---
 files/linux/fdsdk-config.sh | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+)

diff --git a/files/linux/fdsdk-config.sh b/files/linux/fdsdk-config.sh
--- a/files/linux/fdsdk-config.sh
+++ b/files/linux/fdsdk-config.sh
@@ -2840,3 +2840,28 @@ case "$arch" in
         module W83627HF_WDT     # W83627HF + NCT6775/6776/6779/6791/6792 Super-I/O watchdog.
         ;;
 esac
+
+# Bluefin Server ships no /usr/lib/firmware. Wi-Fi (cfg80211, mac80211 and
+# every WLAN driver), Bluetooth, and the AMD, Radeon, Nouveau and Xe GPU
+# drivers need firmware for all or nearly all of the hardware they drive, so
+# they are not built. FSDK's lines above put them and their sub-options in
+# expected-configs: drop from that check exactly the options this block turns
+# off (listed in the build log), so it still catches everything else.
+make -s olddefconfig
+was_set="$(for c in $(sort -u expected-configs); do if has "${c}"; then echo "${c}"; fi; done)"
+remove WLAN
+remove CFG80211
+remove MAC80211
+remove BT
+remove DRM_AMDGPU
+remove DRM_RADEON
+remove DRM_NOUVEAU
+remove DRM_XE
+# A zstd-compressed vmlinuz makes every UKI smaller than gzip does.
+remove KERNEL_GZIP
+enable KERNEL_ZSTD
+make -s olddefconfig
+for c in ${was_set}; do if ! has "${c}"; then echo "${c}"; fi; done >no-firmware-configs
+sed 's/^/Off with the firmware-bound drivers: /' no-firmware-configs
+{ grep -vxF -f no-firmware-configs expected-configs || true; } >expected-configs.new
+mv expected-configs.new expected-configs
-- 
2.43.0
